[Music]  [Applause]  here at the FTTH conference in Valencia  I'm joined by alexandre fonseca the CEO  of Altis in portugal alexandre thanks  for talking to us today now now Portugal  has a great reputation for really  leading the way to some extent with  fiber and with you guys it's not just  the big cities is it no it's true it's  true in fact we have announced back in  2015 our massive rollout of optical  fiber throughout the country that by  2020 we are estimating that we would  reach close to 100% of the population  and this plan is already well underway  and it's been successful we have just  crossed the 4 point 1 million homes fast  mark and in the beginning of 2018 so  it's going ok but it's true what you  said is that there's also a social  impact perspective in our rollout this  means that we have to decide a part of  our investment to dedicate in the  rollout of rural areas we do believe  that children in these rural areas have  the same right and the same  opportunities of children in the big  cities in the video urban areas and to  do that today it's fundamental for these  children to have access to the new era  the new generation of communication  devices so for us it's critical that we  also provide this ultra wideband  connectivity this fiber connectivity to  these very urban non urban areas and  rural areas and this is in fact a part  also of our strategy because we have  decided this year that we want to  continue to be the driving force of the  Portuguese economy but also in the  center of the social responsibility of  everything that goes on in the country  and this fiber rollout is also an  important part with this example that we  are providing we are now present in that  I would say most of the municipalities  in the country including the islands of  Madeira and Azores which are ultra  peripheral areas and we are deploying  optical fiber there as well that's  fantastic and as well as this commitment  to the installation and the services  there's a lot of innovation  there as well yes it is we have laid  down in Portugal in 2015 as soon as all  this acquired virtual Telecom we have  sat down in Portugal our research and  development facilities for the whole  group today not in Lisbon again in a  non-death big urban area in Aveiro which  is a city 300 kilometers north of Lisbon  we have set down our labs all these  slabs as they're called where 700  engineers are developing manufacturing  new solutions between them we can find  new optical fiber solutions so  innovation and this optical fiber  deployment in the country go  hand-in-hand because we do believe that  our ability to influence the technology  roadmap of the deployment is one of the  cornerstones of our success so  innovation is measured for us in the  involvement of these artists labs in the  optical fiber rollout but also its  measured in the new solutions that we  provide that can help this rollout in  the non rural areas that I've just  spoken to you before because sometimes  it's not easy for a massive rollout to  get to a city with 2,000 inhabitants or  1000 inhabitants and innovation is also  being able to run a profitable way to  continue to invest and to roll out fiber  on these small cities as well and in  turn I guess that leads to possibly new  services and solutions that are not  being able to be available before  absolutely I would highlight two  different perspectives one it's a retail  perspective our residential customers  perspective that we know for a fact that  when I would say why the broadband gets  to the home of the customers customers  start to change their usage patterns  they start to use more bandwidth and  with that they become I would say more  demanding in terms of services and  furthermore we know that customers don't  want anymore the Creta party the one  size fits all they want a tailor-made  solution and what we are doing is we are  changing a little bit the way we bundle  products to provide a residential  summers which nowadays is  one-size-fits-all let's be honest we are  changing this into a tailor-made  approach as well taking advantage of the  wider connectivity that they have at  home the second perspective is the  public services perspective it is very  important for a country like Portugal  which is one of the smallest countries  in Europe and with the lowest GDP s one  of the lowest GDP in Europe as well it's  fundamental for us that services public  services like education or health care  can also be modernized and digitalized  then to digitalize the healthcare or the  education system you need to have  connectivity in schools in health  centers in hospitals etc and this is  also a way that we are trying to change  the way we provide products and services  to these public services to allow them  to become digital and by doing this to  improve again the efficiency of our  economy either local either global well  it sounds like you're making major  progress thank you very much for sharing  that with us  thank you very much for digitation and  it's a pleasure to share our experiences  with you always thank you very much so  Altis Portugal using fibre and  innovation to bring brand new products  and services right across the country  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]

